DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260406T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260406T203000
SUMMARY:Keynote Lecture with Dr. Richard Wolff: Scapegoats & Sellouts: Na
 rratives of Capitalist Deception - International Affairs Symposium
DESCRIPTION:Moderated by: Elizabeth Bennett (https://college.lclark.edu/l
 ive/profiles/3380-elizabeth-bennett)  The widening gap of economic inequa
 lity between the capitalist and the worker leads to the rise in anti-fore
 ign and anti-immigrant rhetoric that paves the way for authoritarianism a
 nd fascist regimes. Progressive forces in Western nations have failed to 
 combat this authoritarian wave through properly representing working clas
 s interests\, instead letting tensions boil over. As unions are weakened\
 , austerity is implemented\, and the economy is privatized\, cultural res
 entment is used to channel frustrations of economic exploitation. Without
  a united working class\, authoritarianism is bound to succeed.  Dr. Rich
 ard Wolff is Professor of Economics Emeritus at the University of Massach
 usetts Amherst\, where he taught from 1973 to 2008\, and is currently a V
 isiting Professor in the Graduate Program in International Affairs at the
  New School University in New York City. He earned a BA in History from H
 arvard\, an MA in Economics from Stanford\, and both an MA and PhD in Eco
 nomics from Yale University. Named "America's most prominent Marxist econ
 omist" by The New York Times Magazine\, Dr. Wolff is widely recognized fo
 r his work on economic methodology\, class analysis\, and the structural 
 contradictions of capitalism. He is the co-founder of Democracy at Work\,
  a nonprofit that produces media and live events advocating workplace dem
 ocracy and critiquing capitalism\, and hosts the nationally syndicated pr
 ogram Economic Update.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Moderated by: <a tabindex="0" href="htt
 ps://college.lclark.edu/live/profiles/3380-elizabeth-bennett" target="_bl
 ank" rel="noopener">Elizabeth Bennett</a></p><p>  The widening gap of eco
 nomic inequality between the capitalist and the worker leads to the rise 
 in anti-foreign and anti-immigrant rhetoric that paves the way for author
 itarianism and fascist regimes. Progressive forces in Western nations hav
 e failed to combat this authoritarian wave through properly representing 
 working class interests\, instead letting tensions boil over. As unions a
 re weakened\, austerity is implemented\, and the economy is privatized\, 
 cultural resentment is used to channel frustrations of economic exploitat
 ion. Without a united working class\, authoritarianism is bound to succee
 d.</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_image112260  lw_align_right"><source
  type="image/webp" srcset="https://college.lclark.edu/live/image/scale/2x
