DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260407T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260407T131000
LOCATION:Flanagan Chapel
GEO:45.450821;-122.671419
SUMMARY:Storytelling Event with The Immigrant Story: International Affair
 s Symposium
DESCRIPTION:Co-hosted with The Immigrant Story\, we believe that stories 
 have the power to connect us—to bridge divides\, challenge assumptions\
 , and reshape narratives. We invite you to hear four compelling first-per
 son tales of courage\, fortitude and resilience from Syria\, El Salvador\
 , Bosnia\, and Cambodia by members of our community. These four storytell
 ers from across the globe will share their stories about the journeys tha
 t brought them to the United States\, exploring the complex issues surrou
 nding authoritarianism\, refuge\, and migration. Light lunch will be prov
 ided at the end of the event.Finding Our Way Home Across BordersSonia Pri
 scila Ticas  Born in El Divisadero\, El Salvador\, Sonia Ticas discovered
  her voice at age ten reciting poetry against the backdrop of war and mig
 ration. At eleven\, she journeyed north\, eventually finding refuge in Lo
 s Angeles. Undocumented for years\, she persevered\, earning her PhD in R
 omance Languages from UC Berkeley. Now a tenured professor at Linfield Un
 iversity\, she mentors first-generation students and continues to explore
  the transformative power of literature across borders.A Story I Never Si
 gned Up ForRama Yousef  Rama could not escape the war all around her – 
 bullets\, suicide bombings\, the constant fear of being caught in the mid
 dle of an attack. Expecting the fighting to end soon\, her mother brought
  her to stay with her older married sister in the US. But war tore the fa
 mily apart and Rama soon found herself alone\, a young teenager\, strande
 d in a strange country with no parents or siblings to protect her and no 
 safe place to stay. She had to start making her own decisions.The Heaven 
 BetweenSaron Khut  The last time Saron saw his father was when the Khmer 
 Rouge came and took him away to be killed. Soon afterwards\, they returne
 d for his mother. Still only seven years old\, Saron set off to walk thro
 ugh the jungle alone to find her. It was only when the family\, finally r
 eunited\, was able to flee to a refugee camp in Thailand that Saron could
  be simply a child at last. Recreating the sense of community that sustai
 ned him there has become his life's work.Nothing Else But MiraclesSamir M
 ustafic  Samir Mustafic was born in a small town in Bosnia in 1972. When 
 war between the Bosnians and Serbs broke out\, he joined in the defense o
 f his village. After suffering a severe injury\, he came to the US for su
 rgery and rehab. Later he returned to his village and found his greatest 
 treasure in reconnecting with family and friends. Currently working for t
 he state of Oregon as a software architect\, he takes his wife and teenag
 e twins back for a visit to Bosnia every year.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Co-hosted with The Immigrant Story\, we
  believe that stories have the power to connect us—to bridge divides\, 
 challenge assumptions\, and reshape narratives. We invite you to hear fou
 r compelling first-person tales of courage\, fortitude and resilience fro
 m Syria\, El Salvador\, Bosnia\, and Cambodia by members of our community
 . These four storytellers from across the globe will share their stories 
 about the journeys that brought them to the United States\, exploring the
  complex issues surrounding authoritarianism\, refuge\, and migration. Li
 ght lunch will be provided at the end of the event.</p><p><strong>Finding
  Our Way Home Across Borders</strong><br /><em>Sonia Priscila Ticas</em><
 /p><p>  Born in El Divisadero\, El Salvador\, Sonia Ticas discovered her 
 voice at age ten reciting poetry against the backdrop of war and migratio
 n. At eleven\, she journeyed north\, eventually finding refuge in Los Ang
 eles. Undocumented for years\, she persevered\, earning her PhD in Romanc
 e Languages from UC Berkeley. Now a tenured professor at Linfield Univers
 ity\, she mentors first-generation students and continues to explore the 
 transformative power of literature across borders.</p><p><strong>A Story 
 I Never Signed Up For</strong><br /><em>Rama Yousef</em></p><p>  Rama cou
 ld not escape the war all around her – bullets\, suicide bombings\, the
  constant fear of being caught in the middle of an attack. Expecting the 
 fighting to end soon\, her mother brought her to stay with her older marr
 ied sister in the US. But war tore the family apart and Rama soon found h
 erself alone\, a young teenager\, stranded in a strange country with no p
 arents or siblings to protect her and no safe place to stay. She had to s
 tart making her own decisions.</p><p><strong>The Heaven Between</strong><
 br /><em>Saron Khut</em></p><p>  The last time Saron saw his father was w
 hen the Khmer Rouge came and took him away to be killed. Soon afterwards\
 , they returned for his mother. Still only seven years old\, Saron set of
 f to walk through the jungle alone to find her. It was only when the fami
 ly\, finally reunited\, was able to flee to a refugee camp in Thailand th
 at Saron could be simply a child at last. Recreating the sense of communi
 ty that sustained him there has become his life's work.<br /><br /><stron
 g>Nothing Else But Miracles</strong><br /><em>Samir Mustafic</em></p><p> 
  Samir Mustafic was born in a small town in Bosnia in 1972. When war betw
 een the Bosnians and Serbs broke out\, he joined in the defense of his vi
 llage. After suffering a severe injury\, he came to the US for surgery an
 d rehab. Later he returned to his village and found his greatest treasure
  in reconnecting with family and friends. Currently working for the state
  of Oregon as a software architect\, he takes his wife and teenage twins 
 back for a visit to Bosnia every year.</p><p><br /><picture class="lw_ima
 ge lw_image112631  lw_align_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type
 ="image/webp" srcset="https://www.lclark.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/74/w
 idth/500/height/647/112631_IMMIGRANT_STORYTELLING.webp 2x\, https://www.l
