SUMMARY:Documentary Screening and Discussion with Film Director Abby Mart
 in on Earth's Greatest Enemy in Collaboration with Prison Abolition Club 
 - International Affairs Symposium
DESCRIPTION:Exempt from international climate agreements and rarely scrut
 inized in mainstream reporting\, the Pentagon is the world's single large
 st institutional polluter—spewing carbon\, contaminating water\, and sc
 arring landscapes across the globe. Combining investigative journalism\, 
 striking visuals\, and stories from impacted communities\, this film chal
 lenges audiences to rethink the hidden costs of a global military empire 
 and its planetary consequences. Provocative\, urgent\, and eye-opening\, 
 this is a documentary that will change how you see both the military and 
 environmentalism. The screening will show a 40 minute portion of the film
 \, snacks and refreshments will be provided. Trailer here (https://www.yo
 utube.com/watch?v=rphBWk15_h4).  Abby Martin is an American journalist\, 
 television presenter\, and activist who founded the citizen journalism pl
 atform Media Roots and serves on the board of directors for the Media Fre
 edom Foundation\, which manages Project Censored. She hosted Breaking the
  Set on RT America\, gaining international attention when she publicly co
 ndemned the Russian annexation of Crimea live on air\, before launching T
 he Empire Files \, an investigative documentary and interview series. In 
 2019 she released the feature documentary The Empire Files: Gaza Fights f
 or Freedom . A strong voice on U.S. foreign policy\, militarism\, and cor
 porate power\, Martin brings her lens to her presentation\, Earth's Great
 est Enemy.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Exempt from international climate agree
 ments and rarely scrutinized in mainstream reporting\, the Pentagon is th
 e world's single largest institutional polluter—spewing carbon\, contam
 inating water\, and scarring landscapes across the globe. Combining inves
 tigative journalism\, striking visuals\, and stories from impacted commun
 ities\, this film challenges audiences to rethink the hidden costs of a g
 lobal military empire and its planetary consequences. Provocative\, urgen
 t\, and eye-opening\, this is a documentary that will change how you see 
 both the military and environmentalism. The screening will show a 40 minu
 te portion of the film\, snacks and refreshments will be provided. Traile
 r <a tabindex="0" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rphBWk15_h4" targ
 et="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_im
 age112259  lw_align_right"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://coll
 ege.lclark.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/74/width/400/height/225/112259_abb
 y_2.webp%202x" data-origin="responsive" /><source type="image/jpeg" srcse
 t="https://college.lclark.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/74/width/400/height
 /225/112259_abby_2.jpg%202x" data-origin="responsive" /><img title="abby 
 2" src="https://college.lclark.edu/live/image/gid/74/width/400/height/225
