SUMMARY:Debate with Oren Samet and Susan Stokes on The Authoritarian Thre
 at: Imported or Homegrown? - International Affairs Symposium
DESCRIPTION:As democratic institutions face unprecedented strain\, unders
 tanding the roots of authoritarian resurgence today becomes essential. Th
 is debate confronts a fundamental question: Are today's authoritarian pre
 ssures\, from election interference to democratic backsliding\, primarily
  the product of foreign actors and international forces\, or from domesti
 c pressures such as institutional decay\, elite polarization\, and failin
 g electoral systems?Oren Samet and Susan Stokes  Moderated by: Leah Gilbe
 rt (https://college.lclark.edu/live/profiles/139-leah-gilbert)  Oren Same
 t is the Einstein-Moos Postdoctoral Fellow at Stanford University's Cente
 r on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law. He completed his PhD in
  Political Science at the University of California\, Berkeley and is an i
 ncoming Assistant Professor at Rice University. His research centers on t
 he international dimensions of authoritarian politics and democratization
 \, with a regional focus on Southeast Asia. He was previously based in Ba
 ngkok\, Thailand\, where he was the Research and Advocacy Director of ASE
 AN Parliamentarians for Human Rights  Susan Stokes is the Tiffany and Mar
 garet Blake Distinguished Service Professor and Director of the Chicago C
 enter on Democracy. Her research and teaching interests include democrati
 c theory and how democracy functions in developing societies\, distributi
 ve politics\, and comparative political behavior. Her single and co-autho
 red books include Mandates and Democracy: Neoliberalism by Surprise in La
 tin America (2001)\, Brokers\, Voters\, and Clientelism: The Puzzle of Di
 stributive Politics (2013)\, and Why Bother? Rethinking Participation in 
 Elections and Protests (2019). She is a member of the National Academy of
  Sciences and the American Academy of Arts and Sciences.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As democratic institutions face unprece
 dented strain\, understanding the roots of authoritarian resurgence today
  becomes essential. This debate confronts a fundamental question: Are tod
 ay's authoritarian pressures\, from election interference to democratic b
 acksliding\, primarily the product of foreign actors and international fo
 rces\, or from domestic pressures such as institutional decay\, elite pol
 arization\, and failing electoral systems?</p><h3><em>Oren Samet and Susa
 n Stokes</em></h3><p>  Moderated by: <a tabindex="0" href="https://colleg
 e.lclark.edu/live/profiles/139-leah-gilbert">Leah Gilbert</a></p><p>  Ore
 n Samet is the Einstein-Moos Postdoctoral Fellow at Stanford University's
  Center on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law. He completed his 
 PhD in Political Science at the University of California\, Berkeley and i
 s an incoming Assistant Professor at Rice University. His research center
 s on the international dimensions of authoritarian politics and democrati
 zation\, with a regional focus on Southeast Asia. He was previously based
  in Bangkok\, Thailand\, where he was the Research and Advocacy Director 
 of ASEAN Parliamentarians for Human Rights</p><p style="text-align: left\
 ;">  Susan Stokes is the Tiffany and Margaret Blake Distinguished Service
  Professor and Director of the Chicago Center on Democracy. Her research 
 and teaching interests include democratic theory and how democracy functi
 ons in developing societies\, distributive politics\, and comparative pol
 itical behavior. Her single and co-authored books include <em>Mandates an
 d Democracy: Neoliberalism by Surprise in Latin America (2001)\, Brokers\
 , Voters\, and Clientelism: The Puzzle of Distributive Politics (2013)\, 
 and Why Bother? Rethinking Participation in Elections and Protests (2019)
 .</em> She is a member of the National Academy of Sciences and the Americ
 an Academy of Arts and Sciences.</p>
