LOCATION:JRHH 102
SUMMARY:SOAN Colloquium: JaDee Y. Carathers\, PhD
DESCRIPTION:Oregon's groundbreaking legal model for psilocybin-assisted t
 herapy may be beneficial as gender-affirming care for queer and gender-ex
 pansive people. This qualitative study is based on in-depth interviews wi
 th 15-20 participants (data collection ongoing)\, age 21+ who have receiv
 ed at least one facilitated psilocybin session in OR. Previous research d
 emonstrates that psychedelics have the potential to increase one's sense 
 of gender-based self-acceptance among gender-expansive people (Gaughan et
  al. 2025\; Kruger et al. 2025). Further research is needed to understand
  how the emerging field of regulated psilocybin-assisted therapy (PAT) ma
 y offer support tailored to serve the needs of this population. This stud
 y aims to fill that gap by engaging with the "Oregon model" of PAT to ass
 ess the impact of this modality. Tentative emergent themes include proces
 ses of embodiment through PAT\, engagement with gender archetypes\, heali
 ng traumas related to sexualized interpersonal violence and forms of abus
 e\, connecting with ancestral lineages of strength and resilience\, trans
 formative personal journeys through shame and stigma into personal empowe
 rment and self-actualization.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Oregon's groundbreaking legal model for
  psilocybin-assisted therapy may be beneficial as gender-affirming care f
 or queer and gender-expansive people. This qualitative study is based on 
 in-depth interviews with 15-20 participants (data collection ongoing)\, a
 ge 21+ who have received at least one facilitated psilocybin session in O
 R. Previous research demonstrates that psychedelics have the potential to
  increase one's sense of gender-based self-acceptance among gender-expans
 ive people (Gaughan et al. 2025\; Kruger et al. 2025). Further research i
 s needed to understand how the emerging field of regulated psilocybin-ass
 isted therapy (PAT) may offer support tailored to serve the needs of this
  population. This study aims to fill that gap by engaging with the "Orego
 n model" of PAT to assess the impact of this modality. Tentative emergent
  themes include processes of embodiment through PAT\, engagement with gen
 der archetypes\, healing traumas related to sexualized interpersonal viol
 ence and forms of abuse\, connecting with ancestral lineages of strength 
 and resilience\, transformative personal journeys through shame and stigm
 a into personal empowerment and self-actualization.</p>
