BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260530T100000
LOCATION:Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School
SUMMARY:Trans Form: Grounding into Trans History\, Liberation\, and Hope 
 (In-Person)
DESCRIPTION:At TransActive (https://graduate.lclark.edu/programs/continui
 ng_education/transactive/) we are working to strengthen the systems that 
 surround trans and gender diverse youth. This workshop will help particip
 ants to build a clear understanding of the current political climate we a
 re experiencing\, and discuss how we can leverage effective communication
 \, liberatory practices\, and community engagement to support trans lives
  in this moment.During the workshop participants will:Reflect on the hist
 ory of anti-trans legislation  Take a clear look at the past 10 years of 
 laws affecting transgender people\, both across the United States and her
 e in Oregon\, to better understand the broader context shaping people's l
 ives today.Practice effective communication  Learn strategies for talking
  about these topics with clients and families in ways that are clear\, re
 spectful\, and appropriate for different ages.Explore actionable liberato
 ry practices  Discuss what liberation looks like in action\, bringing tog
 ether social change research with examples of practice established by tra
 nsgender leaders.Map our communities  Create maps of our own communities 
 and explore opportunities to practice community\, leverage change\, and s
 trengthen connections with our people and our values.Following the worksh
 op\, participants will be able to:Explain the social-political processes 
 that have resulted in increased political targeting of transgender and ge
 nder nonconforming communities  Speak to the local political landscape an
 d current protections and challenges in Oregon for transgender communitie
 s  Identify and explain personal hope practice  Understand one to three l
 everage points for enacting change can be utilized to support transgender
  communities in the current political climate    This training is for any
 one supporting trans and gender-diverse youth—including educators\, the
 rapists\, health care providers\, organizers\, parents\, and caregivers�
 �and is designed to deepen understanding of our current moment while embe
 dding liberatory\, trans-centered practices into daily life and work.  Th
 is workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Continuing Education 
 requirement. Click here for more information (http://www.oregon.gov/oblpc
 t/Pages/CCCE.aspx)Event DetailsDate and Time: Saturday\, May 30\, 2026\, 
 9 a.m-4 p.m.Location: Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School\, South ChapelIn
 structors: Carson Varnum\, MEd\, and Finn Johnson&#160\;RegistrationCommu
 nity &amp\; Graduate Student Rate: $60 (no CEUs or PDUs)CEU/PDU Registrat
 ion: $140\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUs\, GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%Cu
 rrent GSEC Supervisors: $112\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUsRegister Online (ht
 tps://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002P22H)Workshop registrat
 ion ends May 24\, 2026  No-cost registrations available for GSEC Faculty 
 and Staff are full. Email cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu) to join 
 the waitlist.Accessibility: We are committed to making our events accessi
 ble to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know you
 r access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@
 lclark.edu)&#160\;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student
  Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for current students only and do not inc
 lude continuing education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsCarson Va
 rnum\, MEd\, (he/him) is a community builder and educator with an experti
 se in sexual health\, sexualities\, peer education\, and community develo
 pment. Carson specializes in building capacity in sex educators\, creatin
 g sexuality education and leadership development programming for adolesce
 nts\, and developing programs that center Queer and Trans communities. Ca
 rson works at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School as TransActives's Head o
 f Education and Outreach\, where he supports transgender\, non-binary\, a
 nd other gender expansive youth by strengthening the systems that surroun
 d them. He received his Masters of Education in Human Sexuality from Wide
 ner University in 2012 and is currently a PhD student in Education and Le
 adership at Pacific University. Carson is a big fan of being on mountains
 \, in rivers\, and with friends.&#160\;Finn Johnson (he/him) is a PhD Can
 didate in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies and Ethnic Studies at Or
 egon State University\, and a law student at Lewis &amp\; Clark Law Schoo
 l. He holds an MA in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies from Oregon S
 tate and a BA in Sexuality\, Gender\, and Queer Studies from Portland Sta
 te University. Before law school\, Finn taught and developed undergraduat
 e courses in Women\, Gender\, and Sexuality Studies at Oregon State\, foc
 using on queer and trans studies. He also publishes scholarship in Critic
 al Race Theory\, Critical Legal Studies\, and LGBTQIA+ STEM Studies. His 
 doctoral research has examined anti-transgender legislation in the U.S. s
 ince 2016. Beyond research\, he has extensive experience as an activist\,
  lobbyist\, and community educator in LGBTQIA+ and reproductive justice p
 olicy.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. Fo
 r the latest on professional development related to your specific interes
 ts\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  At <a href="
 https://graduate.lclark.edu/programs/continuing_education/transactive/" t
 arget="_blank" rel="noopener">TransActive</a> we are working to strengthe
 n the systems that surround trans and gender diverse youth. This workshop
  will help participants to build a clear understanding of the current pol
 itical climate we are experiencing\, and discuss how we can leverage effe
 ctive communication\, liberatory practices\, and community engagement to 
 support trans lives in this moment.</p><h3 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><st
 rong>During the workshop participants will:</strong></h3><h4 style="line-
 height: 1.5\;"><strong>Reflect on the history of anti-trans legislation</
 strong></h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Take a clear look at the past
  10 years of laws affecting transgender people\, both across the United S
 tates and here in Oregon\, to better understand the broader context shapi
 ng people's lives today.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Practi
 ce effective communication</strong></h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  L
 earn strategies for talking about these topics with clients and families 
 in ways that are clear\, respectful\, and appropriate for different ages.
 </p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Explore actionable liberatory 
 practices</strong></h4><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Discuss what liber
 ation looks like in action\, bringing together social change research wit
 h examples of practice established by transgender leaders.</p><h4 style="
 line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Map our communities</strong></h4><p style="li
 ne-height: 1.5\;">  Create maps of our own communities and explore opport
 unities to practice community\, leverage change\, and strengthen connecti
 ons with our people and our values.</p><h4 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><st
 rong>Following the workshop\, participants will be able to:</strong></h4>
 <ul><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Explain the social-political processes
  that have resulted in increased political targeting of transgender and g
 ender nonconforming communities  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\;">Spea
 k to the local political landscape and current protections and challenges
  in Oregon for transgender communities  </li><li style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;">Identify and explain personal hope practice  </li><li style="line-heig
 ht: 1.5\;">Understand one to three leverage points for enacting change ca
 n be utilized to support transgender communities in the current political
  climate  </li></ul><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This training is for 
 anyone supporting trans and gender-diverse youth—including educators\, 
 therapists\, health care providers\, organizers\, parents\, and caregiver
 s—and is designed to deepen understanding of our current moment while e
 mbedding liberatory\, trans-centered practices into daily life and work.<
 /p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cult
 ural Competence Continuing Education requirement. <a href="http://www.ore
 gon.gov/oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx">Click here for more information</a></p><h
 r /><h2><strong>Event Details</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">
 <strong>Date and Time:</strong> Saturday\, May 30\, 2026\, 9 a.m-4 p.m.</
 p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Location:</strong> Lewis &amp\; C
 lark Graduate School\, South Chapel</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><str
 ong>Instructors:</strong> Carson Varnum\, MEd\, and Finn Johnson&#160\;</
 p><h2 style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Registration</strong></h2><p sty
 le="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Community &amp\; Graduate Student Rate:</
 strong> $60 (no CEUs or PDUs)</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>CE
 U/PDU Registration:</strong> $140\, includes 6 CEUs or PDUs\, GSEC Alumni
