BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260622
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260623
LOCATION:Virtual\, and Clackamas Education Service District
SUMMARY:Healthy Cross Cultural Teams - Pre K-12 (Hybrid)
DESCRIPTION:Educators need systematic ways to engage each other on how cu
 lture and race shape all aspects of their work—from curriculum and inst
 ruction to assessment\, feedback\, family and community relationships\, a
 nd cross-cultural collaboration with colleagues.  This week-long course f
 or school-based Pre K-12 educators will help individuals and teams examin
 e their positionality and racial socialization through critical reflectio
 n.  Participants will explore how racial identity development and cultura
 l literacy strengthen relationships with colleagues\, students\, and comm
 unities\, and together build skills for disrupting inequitable systems th
 rough cross-cultural solidarity.Participants in this course will strength
 en their skills around:      Understanding how our racial identity impact
 s our work in schools and the role of racial literacy in creating healthy
  identity development          Developing processes to nurture and grow p
 rincipled relationships across race          Sustaining cross-racial soli
 darity while navigating institutional realities and resistance    Action 
 planning to create a vision and values to help guide the work of cross-ra
 cial teams\, and develop next steps to continue the work back at their si
 tes  Course DetailsDates and Times: Monday-Friday\, June 22-26\, 2026In-P
 erson: June 22\, 24\, 26\, 2026\, 9 a.m.- 4 p.m.\, Clackamas Education Se
 rvice District\, 13455 SE 97th Avenue\, Clackamas\, OR&#160\;Virtual:&#16
 0\;June 23\, 25\, 2026\, 9:30-11:30 a.m.Instructors: Elizabeth Denevi\, P
 hD\, and Jasmine LockeGraduate Continuing Education credit: CEED-866-21\,
  2 semester hours\, $770Limited scholarships available to educators worki
 ng in a Multnomah or Clackamas County School District\, MESD\, or CESD. A
 ll course texts and materials will be provided to all participants at no 
 cost.  Course RegistrationStep 1: Follow the link below to access registr
 ation and scholarship applicationStep 2: CCE will follow up by email with
  credit-bearing course registration and enrollment instructionsRegister o
 nline (https://pdnetworks.soesd.k12.or.us/public/events/view-event/15508)
 About the InstructorsElizabeth Denevi\, PhD\, is an assistant clinical pr
 ofessor at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School of Education and Counseling
 . She has served as a classroom teacher as well as an equity director\, l
 eadership coach\, and director of professional development. She is co-aut
 hor of Learning and Teaching While White: Antiracist Strategies for Schoo
 l Communities (with Jenna Chandler Ward)&#160\;as well as Integrating Edu
 cator Well-Being\, Growth\, and Evaluation: Four Foundations for Leaders 
 (with Lori Cohen)\, both featured titles in Routledge's Equity and Social
  Justice in Education series.Jasmine Locke is an equity-centered educatio
 nal leader and doctoral candidate at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School o
 f Education and Counseling. Her research explores racial literacy\, socia
 lization\, and the educational trajectories of students engaged in Black 
 Student Unions. With a decade of experience as a classroom teacher\, scho
 ol support specialist and equity coach\, she brings deep expertise in cul
 turally responsive pedagogy\, student identity development\, and systems-
 level change. She is featured in Teach for Climate Justice: A Vision for 
 Transforming Education\, highlighting her commitment to joy as a form of 
 resistance.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regularl
 y. For the latest on professional development related to your specific in
 terests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735
 441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Educators ne
 ed systematic ways to engage each other on how culture and race shape all
  aspects of their work—from curriculum and instruction to assessment\, 
 feedback\, family and community relationships\, and cross-cultural collab
 oration with colleagues.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This week-lon
 g course for school-based Pre K-12 educators will help individuals and te
 ams examine their positionality and racial socialization through critical
  reflection.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Participants will explore
  how racial identity development and cultural literacy strengthen relatio
 nships with colleagues\, students\, and communities\, and together build 
 skills for disrupting inequitable systems through cross-cultural solidari
 ty.</p><p><strong>Participants in this course will strengthen their skill
 s around:</strong></p><ul><li style="line-height: 1.5\;"><p>      Underst
 anding how our racial identity impacts our work in schools and the role o
 f racial literacy in creating healthy identity development    </p></li><l
 i style="line-height: 1.5\;"><p>      Developing processes to nurture and
  grow principled relationships across race    </p></li><li style="line-he
 ight: 1.5\;"><p>      Sustaining cross-racial solidarity while navigating
  institutional realities and resistance    </p></li><li style="line-heigh
 t: 1.5\;">Action planning to create a vision and values to help guide the
  work of cross-racial teams\, and develop next steps to continue the work
  back at their sites  </li></ul><hr /><h2 class="p1"><strong>Course Detai
 ls</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Dates and Times:</s
 trong> Monday-Friday\, June 22-26\, 2026</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\; 
