 ener">Register online</a></strong></p><hr /><h2><strong>About the Instruc
 tors</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Elizabeth Denevi\
 , PhD\,</strong> is an assistant clinical professor at Lewis &amp\; Clark
  Graduate School of Education and Counseling. She has served as a classro
 om teacher as well as an equity director\, leadership coach\, and directo
 r of professional development. She is co-author of <em>Learning and Teach
 ing While White: Antiracist Strategies for School Communities</em> (with 
 Jenna Chandler Ward)&#160\;as well as <em>Integrating Educator Well-Being
 \, Growth\, and Evaluation: Four Foundations for Leaders</em> (with Lori 
 Cohen)\, both featured titles in Routledge's Equity and Social Justice in
  Education series.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Jasmine Locke
 </strong> is an equity-centered educational leader and doctoral candidate
  at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School of Education and Counseling. Her r
 esearch explores racial literacy\, socialization\, and the educational tr
 ajectories of students engaged in Black Student Unions. With a decade of 
 experience as a classroom teacher\, school support specialist and equity 
 coach\, she brings deep expertise in culturally responsive pedagogy\, stu
 dent identity development\, and systems-level change. She is featured in 
 <em>Teach for Climate Justice: A Vision for Transforming Education</em>\,
  highlighting her commitment to joy as a form of resistance.</p><div id="
