BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260728T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260728T100000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Supporting Ukrainians Through Trauma Informed\, Culturally Compet
 ent Care (Online)
DESCRIPTION:This workshop invites clinicians into a deeper understanding 
 of the historical and systemic forces shaping Ukrainian mental health. Pa
 rticipants will explore the lasting impacts of colonization\, cultural er
 asure\, forced assimilation\, and ongoing geopolitical trauma\, and how t
 hese experiences can show up in clinical settings.  Participants will gai
 n insight into how these factors influence symptom presentation\, help-se
 eking behaviors\, and engagement in care\, and how a fuller understanding
  of clients' lived experiences can support more accurate assessment and m
 ore effective\, compassionate clinical work.  Together we will consider t
 he roles of individual\, intergenerational\, and collective trauma\, alon
 g with culturally relevant dynamics related to language\, identity\, and 
 safety.  This workshop also provides a space to reflect on implicit bias\
 , microaggressions\, and cultural misunderstandings\, and how they can im
 pact treatment outcomes. Participants will develop strategies to address 
 these dynamics and apply inclusive\, equity-focused approaches in assessm
 ent and treatment planning.  Clinicians will leave the training equipped 
 with practical tools to deliver ethical\, effective\, and culturally resp
 onsive mental health care for Ukrainian clients.Following this workshop\,
  participants will have the ability to:      Describe historical and syst
 emic factors\, including cultural erasure\, affecting Ukrainian mental he
 alth\, and explain their impact on client care          Demonstrate traum
 a-informed and culturally competent interventions when working with Ukrai
 nian clients through case examples          Identify instances of implici
 t bias and microaggressions in clinical scenarios and develop strategies 
 to address them          Apply equity-focused approaches in treatment pla
 nning to ensure inclusive\, ethical\, and effective mental health care   
    This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Continuing Educa
 tion requirement. Click here for more information (http://www.oregon.gov/
 oblpct/Pages/CCCE.aspx)Details &amp\; RegistrationDate and Time: Tuesday\
 , July 28\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-1:30 p.m. PT\, OnlineInstructorss: Valentina Pi
 shchanskaya-Cayanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP\; and Yulia Brockdorf\, RD\, LD\, CDCE
 S\, CST\, NCC\, LPC\, BC-ADM\, BC-TMH\, LMHC\, FAIHM\, LN\, CD\, ACS\, FA
 WMCost and Credits: $105\, includes 4 CEUs. GSEC Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and 
 Clinical Mental Health Supervisors save 20%. $50 student rate.&#160\;Regi
 ster Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002XZAj)Acc
 essibility: We are committed to making our events accessible to all needs
  and abilities. When registering\, please let us know your access needs. 
 Contact us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160
 \;with questions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; r
 egistrations are for current students only and do not include continuing 
 education credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorsValentina Pishchanskaya-C
 ayanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP&#160\;is a Ukrainian licensed marriage and family t
 herapist with extensive experience in clinical practice\, higher educatio
 n\, and nonprofit leadership. She is the owner of Cayanan Counseling and 
 Wellness\, a virtual private practice serving clients across Oregon and W
 ashington. Her practice is dedicated to providing compassionate\, cultura
 lly attuned care with specializations in anxiety\, trauma\, and significa
 nt life adjustments. Valentina is EMDR-trained and integrates evidence-ba
 sed\, trauma-informed approaches to help clients navigate change\, build 
 resilience\, and foster lasting well-being.  As a tenured faculty counsel
 or at Clark College\, Valentina provides psychotherapy\, teaches\, and de
 velops programs to support diverse and underserved populations\, includin
 g Ukrainian students. She is also the Co-Founder and Vice President of DA
 WN Inc.\, where she leads humanitarian and medical aid initiatives in Ukr
 aine and supports refugee integration locally.  Valentina has presented a
 t several conferences\, including the Northwest Regional Equity Conferenc
 e\, on the needs of Ukrainians and the importance of culturally responsiv
 e\, trauma-informed care. She has also spoken to policymakers\, healthcar
 e providers\, and community leaders about the ongoing challenges faced by
  Ukrainians and ways to strengthen systems of support both locally and ab
 road.  Through her clinical\, educational\, and nonprofit work\, Valentin
 a is recognized for advancing resilience\, equity\, and culturally inform
 ed trauma care in both local and international contexts.Yulia Brockdorf\,
  RD\, LD\, CDCES\, CST\, NCC\, LPC\, BC-ADM\, BC-TMH\, LMHC\, FAIHM\, LN\
 , CD\, ACS\, FAWM&#160\;is a Ukrainian-American trauma psychotherapist an
 d registered dietitian specializing in treating complex trauma and cultur
 ally competent care. She is the developer of the first combat psychothera
 py model designed for delivery in active combat zones and has co-authored
  peer-reviewed publications on the psychological impact of drone warfare.
  Ms. Brockdorf provides direct psychotherapy to Ukrainian defenders along
  Ukraine's front lines and has trained mental health professionals\, psyc
 hiatry residents\, and healthcare providers on trauma-informed approaches
  and working with Ukrainian populations.  Ms. Brockdorf developed the tra
 uma and mental health course module Connecting with Ukrainian Refugees: M
 eeting Their Needs with Better Care and Key Service for the Washington St
 ate Department of Health and has lectured at universities and medical sch
 ools on culturally responsive care for displaced populations. Her work ha
 s been published in peer-reviewed literature and she has been featured on
  Oregon's Think Out Loud and other media outlets. She previously served a
 s Board President of the Oregon Board of Licensed Dietitians.  As preside
 nt and co-founder of DAWN\, Inc.\, Ms. Brockdorf has coordinated over $20
  million in medical aid deliveries to Ukraine. She serves on the executiv
 e team of DefenseTech for Ukraine and advocates for U.S. support of Ukrai
 ne at state and federal levels\, including meetings with NATO officials\,
  representatives of the European Parliament\, and members of the Governme
 nt of Ukraine\, and champions both local and international efforts in glo
 bal security.New workshops and trainings are added to our calendar regula
 rly. For the latest on professional development related to your specific 
 interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/47
 35441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  This worksho
 p invites clinicians into a deeper understanding of the historical and sy
 stemic forces shaping Ukrainian mental health. Participants will explore 
 the lasting impacts of colonization\, cultural erasure\, forced assimilat
 ion\, and ongoing geopolitical trauma\, and how these experiences can sho
 w up in clinical settings.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Participant
 s will gain insight into how these factors influence symptom presentation
 \, help-seeking behaviors\, and engagement in care\, and how a fuller und
 erstanding of clients' lived experiences can support more accurate assess
 ment and more effective\, compassionate clinical work.</p><p style="line-
 height: 1.5\;">  Together we will consider the roles of individual\, inte
 rgenerational\, and collective trauma\, along with culturally relevant dy
 namics related to language\, identity\, and safety.</p><p style="line-hei
 ght: 1.5\;">  This workshop also provides a space to reflect on implicit 
 bias\, microaggressions\, and cultural misunderstandings\, and how they c
 an impact treatment outcomes. Participants will develop strategies to add
 ress these dynamics and apply inclusive\, equity-focused approaches in as
 sessment and treatment planning.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Clini
 cians will leave the training equipped with practical tools to deliver et
 hical\, effective\, and culturally responsive mental health care for Ukra
 inian clients.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Following this wo
 rkshop\, participants will have the ability to:</strong></p><ul><li><p st
 yle="line-height: 1.5\;">      Describe historical and systemic factors\,
  including cultural erasure\, affecting Ukrainian mental health\, and exp
 lain their impact on client care    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1
 .5\;">      Demonstrate trauma-informed and culturally competent interven
 tions when working with Ukrainian clients through case examples    </p></
 li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Identify instances of implicit
  bias and microaggressions in clinical scenarios and develop strategies t
 o address them    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">      Apply 
 equity-focused approaches in treatment planning to ensure inclusive\, eth
 ical\, and effective mental health care    </p></li></ul><p style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;">  This workshop may meet the OBLPCT Cultural Competence Con
 tinuing Education requirement. <a href="http://www.oregon.gov/oblpct/Page
 s/CCCE.aspx">Click here for more information</a></p><hr /><h2><strong>Det
 ails &amp\; Registration</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><stro
 ng>Date and Time:</strong> Tuesday\, July 28\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-1:30 p.m. PT
 \, Online</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Instructorss:</strong>
  Valentina Pishchanskaya-Cayanan\, LMFT\, CIMHP\; and Yulia Brockdorf\, R
 D\, LD\, CDCES\, CST\, NCC\, LPC\, BC-ADM\, BC-TMH\, LMHC\, FAIHM\, LN\, 
 CD\, ACS\, FAWM</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Cost and Credits
 :</strong> $105\, includes 4 CEUs. GSEC Alumni\, Adjuncts\, and Clinical 
 Mental Health Supervisors save 20%. $50 student rate.&#160\;</p><p><a cla
